::: {.callout-note}
## What you will do

Starting from the converged `pitzDaily` case of the
[OpenFOAM how-to](../openfoam/how-to.qmd), you will run a **controlled parameter
study**: change *one* numerical ingredient at a time — the **mesh** and the
**discretization scheme** — and measure how the solution responds. This is
exactly how a practising CFD engineer builds trust in a result. You submit your
**case archive plus a few key plots**.

We keep the turbulence model fixed at $k$–$\varepsilon$ throughout; comparing
turbulence models ($k$–$\omega$ SST and others) comes in a later assignment.
:::

## Baseline

Copy your converged case to a fresh working folder and keep it as the reference:

```bash
module load openfoam/v2412
cd /scratch/$USER
cp -r pitzDaily baseline && cd baseline
```

Pick **one quantitative measure** to compare across all runs — e.g. the
reattachment length behind the step, or the streamwise velocity profile on a
vertical line a few step-heights downstream (`postProcess -func singleGraph`).
Every study below is judged by how this measure changes.

## Study A — Mesh sensitivity

Refine the mesh and check whether your answer has stopped changing (grid
independence).

- Copy the baseline to `meshA_fine`. In `system/blockMeshDict`, roughly **double**
  the cell counts (the numbers in the `hex (...) (nx ny nz) ...` lines), then rerun
  `blockMesh` + `simpleFoam`.
- Compare your measure on coarse vs. fine. Does it converge?

::: {.callout-important}
## A — deliverable
Your measure on both meshes, and one sentence: is the baseline mesh fine enough?
:::

## Study B — Discretization scheme (numerical diffusion)

Change **only** the convection scheme for momentum in `system/fvSchemes`, rerunning
each time:

```{.cpp}
div(phi,U)   bounded Gauss upwind;                 // 1st order  — very diffusive
div(phi,U)   bounded Gauss linearUpwind grad(U);   // 2nd order  — baseline
div(phi,U)   Gauss linear;                          // central    — least diffusive
```

Compare the velocity field and your measure across the three. Which smears the
shear layer? Does the central scheme show any oscillations or convergence
trouble?

::: {.callout-important}
## B — deliverable
One comparison plot (field or profile) of the three schemes, and two–three
sentences linking what you see to **numerical diffusion** vs. **boundedness**
from the lectures.
:::

## What to submit

Upload to Crowdmark:

1. **Case archive.** For each run, clean and tar the case (drop the mesh and old
   time steps to keep it small):

   ```bash
   foamCleanTutorials                       # removes time dirs, keeps 0/ + dicts
   cd .. && tar czf baseline.tgz baseline   # repeat per case
   ```

   Submit `baseline.tgz`, `meshA_fine.tgz`, and one archive per scheme in Study B.
2. **Key plots (≤ 5).** Residual convergence for one run; a velocity-magnitude
   field; and the comparison plot(s) of your chosen measure across Studies A and B.
3. **Short discussion (≤ ½ page).** Answer the two deliverable questions above.

::: {.callout-tip}
Change **one thing at a time** and keep the baseline fixed — otherwise you cannot
attribute a difference to any single cause. Note the wall-clock and iteration
count for each run; refinement and higher-order schemes cost more.
:::

::: {.content-visible when-profile="solutions"}
::: {.solution-box}
**Expected trends (instructor notes).**

- **A (mesh):** the coarse baseline is somewhat under-resolved; refining
  lengthens/settles the reattachment as numerical (mesh) diffusion drops. The
  measure should change less between fine and baseline than between baseline and
  a deliberately coarse mesh — the signature of approaching grid independence.
- **B (scheme):** first-order `upwind` adds strong numerical diffusion → the shear
  layer is smeared and the recirculation shortened/washed out. `linearUpwind`
  (2nd order) is sharper. Pure `linear` (central) is least diffusive but may show
  wiggles or slower/failed convergence on this mesh — the accuracy-vs-boundedness
  trade-off from the lecture.

Grading: reward a clean one-variable-at-a-time design, a sensible quantitative
measure, and physical interpretation over "prettier" plots.
:::
:::

